### Moving a card across a board, which is the one thing a PM tool does all day. Every step is
### conditional on the version the previous step returned — which is what stops two people dragging
### the same card from silently overwriting each other.
# @name ReadCard
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}

### todo → doing, with the version the read handed out
# @name Start
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
If-Match: {{ReadCard.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "doing", "estimate_hours": 8 }

### A second editor who read the card at the same time and got there second. Same tag, now stale — so
### their write is refused instead of quietly reverting the estimate above.
# @name SecondEditorLoses
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [412]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
If-Match: {{ReadCard.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "status": "todo", "estimate_hours": 40 }

### Reassign it and finish it, carrying the version the accepted write returned
# @name HandOver
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
If-Match: {{Start.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "assignee_id": "{{GraceId}}", "status": "done" }

### A card can leave its milestone: a `ref` is nullable, so null is a legal PATCH value and not an
### "unchanged" signal. That distinction is worth knowing before you rely on either reading.
# @name DropFromMilestone
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
If-Match: {{HandOver.response.headers.ETag}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "milestone_id": null }

### Where the card ended up
# @name FinalState
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/tasks/{{TaskBId}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyAdmin}}
